response:
 First, we find the direction vector of the line by subtracting the coordinates of the two given points:
\begin{align*}
\left[\begin{array}{r}
x \\
y \\
z \end{array}\right]&=\left[\begin{array}{r}
5 \\
1 \\
-2 \end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{r}
2 \\
2 \\
1 \end{array}\right]\\
&=\left[\begin{array}{r}
3 \\
-1 \\
-3 \end{array}\right]
\end{align*}
Then, we write the coordinates of a point on the line in terms of a parameter $t$:
\begin{align*}
\left[\begin{array}{r}
x \\
y \\
z \end{array}\right]&=\left[\begin{array}{r}
2 \\
2 \\
1 \end{array}\right] + t\left[\begin{array}{r}
3 \\
-1 \\
-3 \end{array}\right]\\
&=\left[\begin{array}{r}
2+3t \\
2-t \\
1-3t \end{array}\right]
\end{align*}
Since the $x$-coordinate of the desired point is 4, we have
\begin{align*}
4&=2+3t\\
2&=3t\\
t&=\frac{2}{3}
\end{align*}
Substituting this value of $t$ into the equation for the $z$-coordinate, we obtain
\begin{align*}
z&=1-3\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)\\
&=1-2\\
&=-1
\end{align*}
Therefore, the $z$-coordinate of the point is $-1$. </answer>

